delta-change "lexical-codecs"
message "LexicalCodecs adds graph-resident bidirectional lexical value codecs"
author "ai"
doc "guide" """
  |# LexicalCodecs: graph-resident lexical value codecs
  |
  |LexicalCodecs is a post-constitution product evolution over LexicalExpressions. It does not modify the frozen F0-F11 bootstrap staircase.
  |
  |## Derivation
  |
  |```text
  |LexicalExpressions + LexicalCodecs.delta = LexicalCodecs
  |```
  |
  |## Purpose
  |
  |LexicalExpressions made lexical recognition graph-resident but lexical AST fields still contained the matched source spelling. LexicalCodecs separates recognition from value semantics:
  |
  |```text
  |source characters
  |      |
  |      v
  |lexer expression graph
  |      |
  |      v
  |matched token
  |      |
  |      v
  |lexer.rule --codec--> lexical codec graph
  |      |
  |      v
  |semantic lexical value
  |```
  |
  |Printing follows the inverse path through the same codec.
  |
  |## Codec vocabulary
  |
  |LexicalCodecs introduces first-class codec nodes:
  |
  |- `lexer.codec.identity`
  |- `lexer.codec.decimal-natural`
  |- `lexer.codec.quoted-string`
  |- `lexer.escape`
  |
  |Each LexicalCodecs lexer rule has a typed `codec` input. String escape mappings are separate graph entities connected to the quoted-string codec by ordered `lexer.codec-escape` edges.
  |
  |No regex or host callback is stored in the graph.
  |
  |## Bool0 codecs
  |
  |### Identifier
  |
  |`lexer.bool0.codec.identifier` uses the identity codec. A recognized identifier such as `alpha_42` produces the semantic value `alpha_42`.
  |
  |### Natural number
  |
  |`lexer.bool0.codec.number` declares radix 10, leading-zero stripping, and canonical zero spelling `0`.
  |
  |```text
  |00042  -> semantic value "42" -> print "42"
  |00000  -> semantic value "0"  -> print "0"
  |```
  |
  |Unicode digit recognition remains a LexicalExpressions lexical-expression concern; the codec maps recognized decimal digits to canonical ASCII decimal value text.
  |
  |### Quoted string
  |
  |`lexer.bool0.codec.string` declares the quote and escape characters. Its escape table is graph data. Decoded characters are stored as canonical Unicode code-point integers, avoiding raw control characters in the delta artifact:
  |
  |```text
  |\"  <-> "
  |\\  <-> \
  |\
  |  <-> newline
  |\	  <-> tab
  |\  <-> carriage return
  |```
  |
  |The AST stores the decoded string value. Printing escapes required characters canonically.
  |
  |## AST representation
  |
  |Earlier LexicalExpressions trees continue to use:
  |
  |```scala
  |FieldValue.Lexeme(sort, constructor, sourceText)
  |```
  |
  |LexicalCodecs trees use:
  |
  |```scala
  |FieldValue.Value(sort, constructor, semanticValue)
  |```
  |
  |This preserves the historical LexicalExpressions product as an executable differential oracle while allowing LexicalCodecs to model true lexical values.
  |
  |## Laws
  |
  |For admitted LexicalCodecs lexical values:
  |
  |```text
  |decode(encode(value)) = value
  |```
  |
  |For accepted source tokens:
  |
  |```text
  |parse(print(parse(source))) = parse(source)
  |```
  |
  |Source spelling may normalize. For example `00042` prints as `42`.
  |
  |Codec definitions fail closed. Duplicate decoded escape values are rejected because they would make canonical encoding ambiguous.
  |
  |## Free changes
  |
  |`DeltaBool0` is extended to admit the LexicalCodecs language-owned kinds:
  |
  |- `lexer.codec.identity`
  |- `lexer.codec.decimal-natural`
  |- `lexer.codec.quoted-string`
  |- `lexer.escape`
  |
  |The generic `replace-preserving-edges` operation can therefore evolve codec semantics without changing Scala parser or lexer code. The successor graph is accepted only when the resulting LexicalCodecs definition validates.
  |
  |## Historical isolation
  |
  |`Language.lexical-expressions` remains the frozen LexicalExpressions product graph. `Language.graph` advances to LexicalCodecs. All LexicalExpressions tests run against `Language.lexical-expressions`, so earlier behavior cannot pass accidentally through the newer implementation.
  |
  """
test "canonical delta contract"
  assert "canonical"
  assert "valid"
  assert "source-roundtrip"
  assert "dependency" "lexical-expressions"
  assert "defines" "delta.bool0.op.remove" "language.change-operator"
